{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The preamble contains a stray closing quotation mark and the dangling phrase \"as further defined below,\" which points to no later definition of \"Agreement.\" The defect is cosmetic and would not change any party's rights.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14},
    {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.06}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002", "issues": [
    {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.13},
    {"issue": "The independent-contractor recital sits against the pervasive COMPANY controls elsewhere (3(ii)(b) policies, 3(ii)(l) inspection, 15(iv)), which a party could argue creates a relationship other than the one declared. The declaration itself is clear, so the tension is one of characterisation rather than contradiction.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003", "issues": [
    {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.15},
    {"issue": "COMPANY's right to \"eliminate any PRODUCT for any reason\" sits against its 3(ii)(e) duty to \"deliver the PRODUCTS to DISTRIBUTOR,\" raising the Lovejoy-type question whether unfettered elimination could nullify the supply obligation. Section 5(i) partly reconciles them by preserving accepted orders.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"AUTHORIZED CHANNEL OF TRADE\" is defined as \"retail grocery store outlet customers only,\" a boundary that is not obviously applied to hybrid or mass-merchandiser food retailers. The definition also fuses the customer class and the territory in one sentence, leaving \"CUSTOMERS\" ambiguous as to whether it means all grocery outlets or only those actually served.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.42},
    {"issue": "The grocery-only definition cannot be squared with 3(ii)(a)(1), which permits sales to Kmart and to \"specific customers as approved by COMPANY,\" and with Exhibit B's express statement that DISTRIBUTOR may distribute to Kmart. Kmart is a mass merchandiser, not a retail grocery outlet.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.46}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Good Industry Practice\" is keyed to standards \"universally accepted and followed in the baking industry,\" a benchmark that is both vague and, as \"universally,\" impossible to satisfy literally. The open-ended \"including, but not limited to\" list leaves the catch-all's reach undefined even though breach of the standard triggers termination under 12(iii)(b).", "type": 1, "prob": 0.44},
    {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.09}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The term clause says the Agreement \"shall continue until December 31, 2010, and shall continue in effect until either party terminates\" on ninety days' notice, which can be read either as a fixed two-year term or as an evergreen agreement terminable at will. The two readings produce opposite results on whether termination after 2010 requires any reason at all.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.58},
    {"issue": "Section 2 lets either party terminate on ninety days' notice with no reason stated, while Section 12(i) permits ninety-day termination only \"for good cause\" or on a change in COMPANY's business; the two cannot both be the exclusive route out. Section 2's opening \"Subject to Section 12\" does not resolve which prevails.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.55}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007", "issues": [
    {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.18},
    {"issue": "COMPANY's power to \"determine the TERRITORY and AUTHORIZED CHANNEL OF TRADE\" unilaterally is hard to reconcile with Section 1(iii) and Exhibit A, which fix the TERRITORY by a signed schedule, and with Section 15(iii)'s requirement that amendments be by written instrument executed by both parties. Read literally it would let COMPANY erase the distributor's territory entirely, the Lovejoy problem.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.44}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The service obligations \"will consist of, but are not limited to\" four enumerated items, leaving the open-ended residue of required in-store service undefined; and \"guaranteed sales\" is defined only as credit \"for all stale and damaged products.\" The paragraph numbering also runs (1) then (ii), obscuring whether the service duty is a separate obligation or a subpart of the sales restriction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.41},
    {"issue": "The permission to sell to Kmart and to \"specific customers as approved by COMPANY\" conflicts with Section 1(iii), which limits the AUTHORIZED CHANNEL OF TRADE to \"retail grocery store outlet customers only,\" and with 3(ii)(c)'s bar on any other channel of trade. Which text controls a Kmart or approved non-grocery sale is unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.38}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009", "issues": [
    {"issue": "After two specific prohibitions the clause adds that DISTRIBUTOR \"must meet certain other guidelines that the COMPANY may determine in its discretion from time to time,\" an unbounded catch-all appended to an enumerated list. Neither \"Wholesaler operations\" nor \"bid process\" is defined.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.43},
    {"issue": "The ban on wholesaler operations without in-store service is in tension with 15(ii)(a), which expressly permits DISTRIBUTOR to conduct business through subdistributors. Whether servicing stores through an independent distributor is prohibited \"Wholesaler operations\" is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.34}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The obligation to adhere to standards \"as shall from time to time be promulgated, altered, amended or modified by COMPANY\" has no limit and no defined content, so the scope of the promise cannot be fixed from the text. The sentence is also broken by stray subsection markers, obscuring where the obligation ends.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38},
    {"issue": "A duty to follow policies COMPANY may unilaterally change conflicts with Section 15(iii), which permits modification of the Agreement \"only by a written instrument duly executed by the parties.\" A court would have to decide whether a policy change is an amendment.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.36}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The bar on selling to \"retail stores serviced by the Kellogg's Snacks DSD division\" identifies the excluded stores by a shifting factual condition the DISTRIBUTOR cannot verify, and the clause fixes no time at which service is measured. The prohibition's boundary therefore moves.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38},
    {"issue": "The disclaimer that DISTRIBUTOR has no exclusive rights to \"any stores or designated geography\" sits against Exhibit A's grant of a defined TERRITORY and Exhibit B's grandfather of existing customers. Whether any protected book of business survives is unclear.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.31}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The prohibition on selling \"in any AUTHORIZED CHANNEL OF TRADE other than as stated herein\" is a drafting error: the authorized channel is by definition the permitted one, so the sentence forbids what it elsewhere allows and should read \"any channel of trade other than\" the authorized one. The 30-day effectiveness clause is also mangled (\"effective thirty days of receipt\").", "type": 1, "prob": 0.47},
    {"issue": "COMPANY's right to revise Exhibit B \"without notice in the sole discretion of COMPANY\" conflicts with Section 15(iii), which allows modification of the Agreement and its exhibits only by a written instrument executed by both parties, and with Exhibit B's own grandfather clause for existing customers. Which text governs a unilateral channel change is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.44}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause conditions COMPANY's duty to use \"commercially reasonable efforts\" on \"sales which are in conflict with Paragraph 3(a)(i) above,\" but there is no Paragraph 3(a)(i) in this Agreement. The cross-reference error leaves the trigger for the obligation unidentifiable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.47},
