  {"clause_id": "c055", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"[G]ood cause\" is defined as breach \"not cured within those times specified below,\" yet the clause simultaneously requires ninety days' written notice, so it is unclear whether the ninety days runs before or after the cure period. \"[A] change in COMPANY's business that results in COMPANY no longer using Independent DSDD distributors in this territory\" is a further undefined trigger resting entirely on COMPANY's own conduct.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.58},
    {"issue": "Requiring good cause and ninety days' notice cannot be reconciled with Section 2, which lets either party terminate on ninety days' notice without any cause, nor with 12(ii) and 12(iii), which permit immediate termination or termination after a five-business-day cure period. The three termination regimes give inconsistent answers to the same facts.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.60}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c056", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Make a material change in the control of DISTRIBUTOR'S company that is unacceptable to COMPANY\" makes the trigger turn on COMPANY's unexpressed subjective judgment, and neither \"material change in control\" nor \"unacceptable\" is defined. Item (f)'s \"threatens to do substantial harm to the COMPANY'S business or commercial reputation\" is equally open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.45},
    {"issue": "Immediate termination for an attempted assignment sits against 15(ii)(a), which declares any such attempted assignment simply \"void and without effect,\" and against 12(i)'s definition of good cause as an uncured breach requiring ninety days' notice. The remedies for the same act differ across the instrument.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.42}
